  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 243 AM CDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nAn area of showers remains ongoing across east central MN into \nall of west central WI, driven by southwesterly 850 mb moisture \ntransport across southern MN. Rain totals have been pretty \nimpressive with MRMS suggesting three streaks of 3/4\"+ amounts -\nSt. Cloud and east, Willmar and east, and from Le Sueur Co and\neast. Estimates are even higher across central Meeker and\nsouthern Wright Counties where near an inch and half has likely\nfallen. The moisture transport will veer with time and should \nallow the showers to focus more in Wisconsin before things dry \nout for the rest of the day. Low clouds and light fog have \ndeveloped across southern and western MN. It should take several\nhours to begin clearing out today, possibly not until late \nafternoon with a healthy inversion above 5 kft, but forecast \nsoundings do show it occurring eventually.\n\nAttention then turns to the heavy rain threat Friday. The system\nis currently over the Four Corners/southern Rockies and is\nbeing driven north by an upper low over the West Coast and the\nvery large and intense ridge across the south. It will begin to\ncrest the ridge and reach the Upper Midwest late tonight and\nFriday. Not much has changed with respect to the overall idea of\nthis system's evolution, but now that CAMs are beginning to \ncapture the event, some smaller scale details are becoming \nclearer. By early Friday morning, a large shield of showers will\nbe advancing into central and southern MN, with the heaviest \nrates over eastern SD. As the day progresses, this shield will \npush east across the area and then weaken late in the day. \nHeavy rainfall totals (1-2+ inches) are likely where the \nelongated shield persists the longest. Most models highlight an \narea from west central MN towards the metro as the highest \nlikelihood. That round will be driven primarily by mid level \nforcing. A second round tied to the surface warm front should \ndevelop later in the day farther south across south central and \nsoutheast MN into northeast IA. There will be potential for very\nheavy totals in this area given that training thunderstorms is \na possibility with storm motions parallel to the surface front. \nHow far north this develops and whether it will intersect any \nhard hit areas from the first round is the main question. Given \nlarge spread in the guidance with members continually shifting \naround, a blended approach for QPF remains appropriate. NBM may\nbe too high in some places and not high enough in others, but \nit does sufficiently highlight the areas of most concern - the \nsouthern portion of the metro and south/east from there into \nnortheast IA and southwest WI.\n\nThe front will settle south behind the Friday system, bringing \ndrier weather Saturday. The upper low out west will make slow\nprogress east and bring another chance of showers late Sunday\ninto Monday.",
